Improvements for next run
I played the 5 day option but I went to day 6 with continued play. 5 days should be 5-days max and there should only be 2 scenarios a day for both game play modes
player mode should not show what number roll they got to determine the difficulty of the mini game
there should be a brief explanation to tell the player why they were injured
there needs to be some incentive to complete the extra task
if the player was injured by an infected, there should be some opportunity to treat the would within the next 3 turns
if the player has maxed out the amount of times they can syphon gas, that option should not even show up for them anymore not even greyed out. maybe another scenario for when the player runs out of fuel and has maxed out the number of times they can syphone gas
Make choices feel less hidden. Many effects only show up later, so players can’t tell why they died. A short dashboard flash after each choice would help, like “−10 water, +1 h, injury dodged.”
Callback scenes could say which earlier choice triggered them (“Because you helped the couple on Day 2…”).
Make passengers worth having. Right now they mostly cost food and water. Each one could also bring a perk while they ride, such as a mechanic who repairs the car, a nurse who heals, or a lookout who lowers the danger. Then picking someone up is a real trade-off instead of a pure act of kindness. But they may also add nothing it can be a random roll
Player has an option to replay a mini game if they lost (player can play each mini game a maximum of 2 times)
Add replay hooks.
Unlockable starting perks after a win, like an extra gallon or the coat.
A route-completion map on the title screen showing which cities you’ve beaten in each mode.
Badges for different endings, since there are already 7 endings and victory types.
Side missions could get the four-choice treatment, for example “Take it on,” “Not this trip,” “Take it, but ask for supplies up front,” and “Point them to someone else.”
Add a short how-to-play card the first time someone plays.
Add sound cues for low supplies, like a heartbeat when health is under 25%.
Add a “what if” replay at the end. On the end screen, show the player’s two or three biggest turning points and what the odds would have been if they’d picked differently. It uses the same random seed, so it’s a fair comparison. It teaches cause and effect, and it gets people to play again.
